 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "dover_walter_tull",
   "title": "Walter Tull",
   "text": "Walter Tull was born in Folkestone, just along the coast, in 1888, the son of a carpenter from Barbados. Orphaned young, he became a professional footballer with Tottenham Hotspur and Northampton Town, joined the army in 1914 and became one of the first Black officers in the British Army. He was killed in France on 25 March 1918.",
   "people": "black_britain"
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "Dover's market square has seen Romans, Saxons, Normans, smugglers and day-trippers. Under it lie the remains of the Roman port of Dubris, whose harbour sat at the mouth of the River Dour where the town is now.",
   "plaque": "dover_market_square",
   "title": "The Market Square"
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 26,
   "text": "Part of a Roman hotel for officials crossing the Channel, built about 200 AD and found in 1970. Its walls still carry painted plaster, the best preserved of their kind in Britain, and it has underfloor heating. The Romans would have hated to be cold in Kent too.",
   "plaque": "dover_painted_house",
   "people": "romans",
   "title": "The Roman Painted House"
  },
  {
   "x": 22,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "In 1992 roadworkers found a boat buried in the mud, made of oak planks sewn together with yew withies, about 3,500 years old. It is one of the oldest seagoing boats ever found, and it may have crossed the Channel. It is in the museum now, in a very large glass case.",
   "plaque": "dover_bronze_age_boat",
   "title": "The Dover Bronze Age Boat"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "One of Dover's oldest pubs. By tradition, swimmers who make it across the Channel sign their names on its walls and ceiling, and there are thousands now, from all over the world. You can only sign if you've swum it. Being thirsty does not count.",
   "plaque": "dover_white_horse",
   "title": "The White Horse"
  },
  {
   "x": 12,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "On 24 August 1875 Captain Webb walked into the sea here, covered in porpoise oil, and swam to France: the first person to swim the Channel without help. It took him nearly 22 hours, because the tide kept pushing him back. The memorial on the seafront remembers him.",
   "plaque": "dover_captain_webb",
   "title": "Captain Matthew Webb"
  },
  {
   "x": 35,
   "y": 39,
   "text": "The narrowest crossing to the continent, only 21 miles to Cap Gris-Nez, and the busiest ferry port in Britain, with millions of passengers and lorries a year. On a clear day you can see France from the cliffs, and France can see you.",
   "plaque": "dover_port",
   "title": "The Port of Dover"
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "Dover was one of the Cinque Ports, said 'sink', a league of Kent and Sussex harbours that gave the medieval kings ships in return for privileges. Their head is the Lord Warden of the Cinque Ports, an honour given to people such as Winston Churchill and the Queen Mother.",
   "plaque": "dover_cinque_ports",
   "title": "The Cinque Ports"
  },
  {
   "x": 10,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "Huge forts dug into the hill against a French invasion that never came, begun during the Napoleonic Wars. The Grand Shaft is a triple spiral staircase cut down through the cliff in 1806 so soldiers could reach the town fast: three staircases, twisting round each other.",
   "plaque": "dover_western_heights",
   "title": "The Western Heights"
  },
  {
   "x": 47,
   "y": 13,
   "text": "Called the Key of England: whoever held it held the shortest way in. Henry II built the great keep in the 1180s, and the castle was garrisoned for over 800 years, right up to 1958. It has seen sieges, Napoleonic barracks and the Second World War.",
   "plaque": "dover_castle",
   "title": "Dover Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 41,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "Next to the castle church stands a Roman lighthouse, built in the first or second century AD to guide ships into Dubris. It is one of the tallest Roman structures left standing in Britain. Its partner on the Western Heights has all but gone.",
   "plaque": "dover_pharos",
   "title": "The Roman Pharos"
  },
  {
   "x": 56,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "In May 1940 the British army was trapped at Dunkirk. From tunnels in these cliffs, Vice-Admiral Bertram Ramsay planned the evacuation, Operation Dynamo. Between 26 May and 4 June about 338,000 soldiers were brought home, helped by hundreds of little ships: ferries, fishing boats, pleasure steamers and private yachts.",
   "plaque": "dover_operation_dynamo",
   "title": "Operation Dynamo"
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 4,
   "text": "On 25 July 1909 Louis Bleriot flew across the Channel from Calais in his little monoplane and crash-landed in a meadow just north of the castle: the first aeroplane flight across the Channel. A memorial in the grass is shaped like his aeroplane.",
   "plaque": "dover_bleriot",
   "title": "Bleriot's Landing"
  },
  {
   "x": 44,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "Chalk, up to 110 metres high, made from the limy skeletons of tiny sea algae that settled on a warm sea floor between about 100 and 66 million years ago. Fulmars and kittiwakes nest on the ledges, and peregrines hunt along them. For returning sailors and soldiers, they were the first sight of home.",
   "plaque": "dover_white_cliffs",
   "title": "The White Cliffs"
  },
  {
   "x": 52,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "In 1942 Vera Lynn sang that there would be bluebirds over the White Cliffs of Dover. It is a beautiful song, and there are no bluebirds in Britain at all: they are American birds, and the song was written by Americans. Swallows, perhaps.",
   "plaque": "dover_bluebirds",
   "theme": "arts",
   "title": "Bluebirds over the White Cliffs?"
  },
  {
   "x": 58,
   "y": 14,
   "plaque": "dover_chain_home",
   "title": "Chain Home",
   "text": "On 26 February 1935, near Daventry, Robert Watson-Watt and Arnold Wilkins showed that radio waves bouncing off an RAF bomber could be picked up on the ground. By 1939 a chain of radar stations, Chain Home, watched the coast. In the Battle of Britain in 1940 it told fighter pilots where to meet the raiders. Some of the tall masts of the Swingate station still stand on the cliffs above the town."
  },
  {
   "x": 29,
   "y": 40,
   "plaque": "landsky_shipping_forecast",
   "title": "The Shipping Forecast",
   "text": "Thirty-one sea areas, read on BBC radio in a set order that starts with Viking and ends at Southeast Iceland, each with its wind, its weather and its visibility. You are standing in sea area Dover, between Thames and Wight. Gale means force 8 on the scale Francis Beaufort of County Meath drew up for the Royal Navy in 1805. The late broadcast, at twelve minutes to one, follows a tune called Sailing By, and a great many people who have never been to sea listen to it in bed."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 32,
   "plaque": "landsky_chalk_channel",
   "title": "How the Channel was cut",
   "text": "Until a few hundred thousand years ago a ridge of chalk joined Kent to France and Britain was a corner of the continent. A vast lake of meltwater built up behind it in the North Sea basin and, about 450,000 years ago, burst through in a megaflood that carved the Strait of Dover; a second flood about 160,000 years ago finished the job. The scars of those floods still lie on the sea floor between here and Calais."
  }
 ],
